  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 220 AM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nMid-level ridging remains in place over the Central and Southern \nPlains early this morning, but change looms as a potent shortwave \nejects east along the US/Canada border later today. In the meantime, \nthe pressure gradient has tightened across the area and elevated \nsurface winds have kept the boundary layer mixed, leading to warm \novernight temperatures holding in the mid 70s to near 80 degrees. \nDeep mixing and 850mb temperatures of 25-27 degrees C will result in \nanother hot day with highs in the 100-105 degree range for most. \nDewpoints will likely mix out into the low 60s across north central \nand central Kansas while dewpoints hold in the mid to upper 60s \nfarther east. All-in-all, heat index values will reach 103-109 \ndegrees this afternoon. The Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect \nthrough 7 PM this evening. \n\nThe aforementioned shortwave across the northern US will squash the \nmid-level ridge and push a cold front into the area this evening \ninto Wednesday. Guidance is in good agreement in this front pushing \ninto north central Kansas by around 5 PM. The best forcing is \ndisplaced north of the front, but frontal convergence will be \nsufficient to generate showers and storms along and behind the \nboundary. Shear is progged to be marginal (20kts), but storms could \nproduce damaging wind gusts around 60 MPH given inverted-V soundings \nand high DCAPE values. Isentropic ascent and weak passing energy \nwill continue scattered showers and storms overnight into Wednesday \nmorning as the boundary slowly sags south across the area. A cooler \nairmass builds in behind the front, leading to a less hot day with \nhighs in the 80s to low 90s on Wednesday. The pre-frontal \nenvironment will still be hot and favorable for some marginally \nsevere storms by Wednesday afternoon, and guidance is again in good \nagreement that the front will still be across east-central Kansas by \nthe early afternoon. Thus, areas south and east of the Kansas \nTurnpike could see a couple of strong to marginally severe storms. \nHighs in this area could also reach into the mid to upper 90s with \nheat index values around 100 degrees. Will hold off on any extension \nof heat headlines as a faster front and/or precipitation and cloud \ncover could keep conditions cooler. \n\nThe front will push through the area by Wednesday evening, bringing \nin much anticipated cooler temperatures for Thursday! Highs in the \n80s on Thursday will warm slightly into the upper 80s and low 90s on \nFriday before another shortwave and surface front move through the \narea Friday night into Saturday. There is a signal for an MCS to \ndevelop and push across the area during this timeframe, with enough \ninstability and shear to support some strong to severe storms. Will \nneed to monitor this potential over the coming days. Passing waves \nin the quasi-zonal flow lead to continued chances for precipitation \ninto early next week. Long-range guidance has been suggesting a \nstrong cold front push through in the Monday-Tuesday timeframe. \nTemperature spreads are quite large still, but there is potential \nfor temperatures to fall below-normal behind this front, bringing\na more fall-like feel to the area. Uncertainty remains, so stay\ntuned!",
